WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.750 --> 00:00:05.183
The year: 1974.

00:00:06.250 --> 00:00:10.043
The location: outer space
between Venus and Mercury.

00:00:12.770 --> 00:00:16.300
NASA's Mariner 10 probe
had a serious problem.

00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:18.320
Because of some unplanned maneuvers,

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:22.000
they were running much lower
on propellant than expected

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:25.210
and in order to maintain
control of the spacecraft,

00:00:25.210 --> 00:00:27.113
NASA had to come up with a workaround.

00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:30.960
If they didn't do something quickly,

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:33.870
the probe could miss Mercury altogether.

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:36.643
That's when engineers came
up with an ingenious plan.

00:00:37.815 --> 00:00:38.970
And what they ended up doing

00:00:38.970 --> 00:00:43.460
was using the solar
panels of the spacecraft

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:46.670
and just angling those panels
with respect to the sun

00:00:46.670 --> 00:00:48.800
and letting sunlight pressure

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:51.373
move and position the
spacecraft as needed.

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:54.610
And it worked.

00:00:54.610 --> 00:00:57.740
Mariner 10 became the first
probe to fly by Mercury,

00:00:57.740 --> 00:01:00.513
giving humans the first
close-up images of the planet.

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:05.150
This close call showed that solar pressure

00:01:05.150 --> 00:01:07.740
could be used to keep a probe on course

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:09.543
just like a sailboat uses the wind.

00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:13.660
So NASA was able to successfully

00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:16.030
sail with Mariner 10,

00:01:16.030 --> 00:01:17.810
even though that was not part

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:19.360
of the original mission design.

00:01:29.550 --> 00:01:31.700
Solar sailing uses an existing,

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:34.213
almost limitless energy
supply from the sun.

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:36.920
If you have a light on right now,

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:38.490
or are in the sunlight,

00:01:38.490 --> 00:01:40.420
you might be surprised
to know that that light

00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:41.433
is pushing you.

00:01:43.090 --> 00:01:44.550
Well believe it or not, sunlight actually

00:01:44.550 --> 00:01:46.440
does exert a force on us.

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:48.120
If you go outside on a bright, sunny day,

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:50.660
you're probably feeling the equivalent

00:01:50.660 --> 00:01:53.380
of maybe the same amount of force

00:01:53.380 --> 00:01:56.040
as a very light feather might exert

00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:58.090
resting in the palm of your hand.

00:01:58.090 --> 00:02:00.790
It's not very much and
we don't even notice it

00:02:00.790 --> 00:02:03.380
because even the slightest breezes

00:02:03.380 --> 00:02:07.280
will easily overwhelm our sense of

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:08.280
being pushed.

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:10.480
But in space, it's a very different story

00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:13.350
because there is no ground
to anchor yourself against.

00:02:13.350 --> 00:02:15.460
There is no other wind or breezes

00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:17.520
or other forces acting on you.

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:19.670
So, ever so slowly

00:02:19.670 --> 00:02:22.840
but surely that sunlight
pressure will build up

00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:25.943
and will very gradually
accelerate you away.

00:02:31.210 --> 00:02:32.910
Solar sailing was first written about

00:02:32.910 --> 00:02:35.190
by Johannes Kepler in 1610

00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:37.540
who thought a space sail
might one day capture sunlight

00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:39.390
the way a boat sail catches the wind.

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:43.400
Although he didn't fully
understand the mechanism

00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:44.920
to make this a reality,

00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:48.210
it was later theorized by
physicist James Clerk Maxwell,

00:02:48.210 --> 00:02:49.900
who mathematically showed that sunlight

00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:52.803
carries momentum that
exerts pressure on objects.

00:02:55.540 --> 00:02:57.750
If you get in the near vacuum of space

00:02:57.750 --> 00:03:01.400
and you have a low-mass, low-weight object

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:03.500
and a shiny, reflective sail

00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:05.270
that the light can bounce off of,

00:03:05.270 --> 00:03:07.880
you can actually use it
to propel a spacecraft.

00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:09.523
Or at least that's the theory.

00:03:11.930 --> 00:03:14.410
Solar sailing offers a possible solution

00:03:14.410 --> 00:03:17.880
to one of the largest obstacles
facing space missions today:

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:20.170
a limited fuel supply.

00:03:20.170 --> 00:03:22.660
Once you use up all of your propellant,

00:03:22.660 --> 00:03:24.770
you've got nothing else to push with.

00:03:24.770 --> 00:03:27.513
And that's a fundamental
limitation of rockets.

00:03:28.890 --> 00:03:30.350
But with successful solar sailing

00:03:30.350 --> 00:03:32.500
demonstration missions by the Japanese

00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:35.540
Aerospace Exploration Agency, NASA,

00:03:35.540 --> 00:03:38.240
and most recently the Planetary Society,

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:40.630
missions could now be longer, cheaper,

00:03:40.630 --> 00:03:44.223
quicker and go farther out
into space than ever before.

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:49.610
It really opens up the
solar system for exploration

00:03:49.610 --> 00:03:53.330
on a scale that we cannot come close to

00:03:53.330 --> 00:03:54.883
with our current technology.

00:03:59.310 --> 00:04:03.340
Astronomers sometimes
say that space is curved.

00:04:03.340 --> 00:04:04.780
People like Carl Sagan,

00:04:04.780 --> 00:04:07.030
the co-founder of the Planetary Society,

00:04:07.030 --> 00:04:09.890
saw the potential it
had for future missions.

00:04:09.890 --> 00:04:10.980
Now what will this do?

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:13.470
Well, it takes you to where you wanna go.

00:04:13.470 --> 00:04:15.170
So one mission that's being talked about

00:04:15.170 --> 00:04:18.145
is to rendezvous with Halley's Comet.

00:04:18.145 --> 00:04:20.405
I've always wanted to do that.

00:04:20.405 --> 00:04:22.190
He would inspire a generation

00:04:22.190 --> 00:04:25.490
to make his dream a reality
years after his death.

00:04:25.490 --> 00:04:27.840
But the Planetary
Society's first solar sail,

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:30.300
Cosmos 1, never made it into orbit

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:32.373
after a rocket failure in 2005.

00:04:33.310 --> 00:04:35.890
It was the Japanese Aerospace
Exploration Agency's

00:04:35.890 --> 00:04:38.120
IKAROS that became the
first to demonstrate

00:04:38.120 --> 00:04:40.340
solar sailing in 2010.

00:04:40.340 --> 00:04:43.530
And a year later, NASA's
NanoSail-D did the same

00:04:43.530 --> 00:04:44.493
in low-earth orbit.

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:48.340
But it's the development
of even more recent

00:04:48.340 --> 00:04:51.090
technologies that are starting
to enable real missions.

00:04:53.080 --> 00:04:55.450
The key enabling technology
behind solar sailing

00:04:55.450 --> 00:04:57.120
isn't so much the sail,

00:04:57.120 --> 00:04:59.400
it's making the payload

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:02.130
as small and lightweight as possible.

00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:04.510
And in recent years, we've developed

00:05:04.510 --> 00:05:06.990
what's called CubeSat technology.

00:05:06.990 --> 00:05:10.100
We even have what's
called NanoSat technology.

00:05:10.100 --> 00:05:12.030
And these are tiny satellites

00:05:12.030 --> 00:05:15.200
that can weigh less than a kilogram

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:18.100
and contain all of the
electronics necessary

00:05:18.100 --> 00:05:19.980
to conduct a mission.

00:05:19.980 --> 00:05:23.140
Everything from your
avionics, your communication,

00:05:23.140 --> 00:05:25.760
attitude control, navigation,

00:05:25.760 --> 00:05:30.430
everything all baked onto
a few chips of silicon.

00:05:30.430 --> 00:05:33.360
So these CubeSats,
coupled with a solar sail,

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:37.173
make for a very effective
yet very low cost payload.

00:05:41.170 --> 00:05:42.700
This combination of solar sailing

00:05:42.700 --> 00:05:45.140
and lightweight craft, when used together,

00:05:45.140 --> 00:05:47.280
could make space exploration cheaper

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:49.530
and more accessible to everyone.

00:05:49.530 --> 00:05:51.610
And the Planetary Society set out to prove

00:05:51.610 --> 00:05:53.236
that it could be a practical option.

00:05:54.580 --> 00:05:57.390
In 2019, their super lightweight CubeSat

00:05:57.390 --> 00:06:00.260
the size of a loaf of
bread, called LightSail 2,

00:06:00.260 --> 00:06:02.563
launched aboard the SpaceX Falcon Heavy.

00:06:07.210 --> 00:06:12.050
So LightSail 2 launched as a
CubeSat that was this size.

00:06:12.050 --> 00:06:14.350
This is a one-to-one cardboard version.

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:17.550
And once it got in orbit,

00:06:17.550 --> 00:06:18.383
it opened up

00:06:19.370 --> 00:06:22.600
solar panels exposing inside

00:06:22.600 --> 00:06:23.780
the sail material

00:06:23.780 --> 00:06:27.290
and some of the
instrumentation, the cameras.

00:06:27.290 --> 00:06:28.710
Then we deployed the sail,

00:06:28.710 --> 00:06:30.940
which is about the size of a boxing ring,

00:06:30.940 --> 00:06:32.980
32 square meters,

00:06:32.980 --> 00:06:36.400
and it deployed these
four triangular sails

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:39.253
that come out of the loaf of bread.

00:06:40.160 --> 00:06:44.370
The material of the sail itself is Mylar,

00:06:44.370 --> 00:06:48.510
so like Mylar balloons
but actually thinner.

00:06:48.510 --> 00:06:49.760
It's about

00:06:50.600 --> 00:06:52.660
four microns in

00:06:52.660 --> 00:06:56.620
width, micron being one
millionth of a meter,

00:06:56.620 --> 00:06:59.390
so it's very thin and
to keep it from ripping,

00:06:59.390 --> 00:07:01.700
it's got so-called rip stops,

00:07:01.700 --> 00:07:05.253
which are strings that
are in a hash pattern.

00:07:06.380 --> 00:07:07.530
Light from the sun,

00:07:07.530 --> 00:07:09.260
which is made up of photons,

00:07:09.260 --> 00:07:11.940
hit the shiny Mylar
material carrying momentum,

00:07:11.940 --> 00:07:13.673
bouncing off and causing a push.

00:07:15.130 --> 00:07:18.670
The team then used a momentum
wheel to steer the spacecraft.

00:07:18.670 --> 00:07:20.900
LightSail 2 was the first ever mission

00:07:20.900 --> 00:07:23.650
to successfully maneuver in
space using solar sailing.

00:07:26.590 --> 00:07:28.200
The main goal with LightSail 2

00:07:28.200 --> 00:07:30.990
was to demonstrate
controlled solar sailing

00:07:30.990 --> 00:07:34.550
in a small spacecraft,
specifically CubeSat,

00:07:34.550 --> 00:07:36.223
and we did accomplish that.

00:07:37.760 --> 00:07:39.510
And it's still sailing.

00:07:39.510 --> 00:07:41.538
But this is only the beginning.

00:07:41.538 --> 00:07:43.860
In the next few years, NASA's Artemis 1

00:07:43.860 --> 00:07:45.430
will head to the moon and on board

00:07:45.430 --> 00:07:46.940
will be a solar sailing mission called

00:07:46.940 --> 00:07:49.573
the Near Earth Asteroid
Scout or NEA Scout.

00:07:50.670 --> 00:07:53.370
Once in space, it will
separate from Artemis,

00:07:53.370 --> 00:07:55.410
and travel to an asteroid
within three years

00:07:55.410 --> 00:07:56.443
after its launch.

00:07:57.920 --> 00:08:00.180
So we actually have an
agreement with NASA.

00:08:00.180 --> 00:08:02.010
So we're working directly

00:08:02.010 --> 00:08:05.270
with their Near Earth Asteroid
Scout solar sail mission

00:08:05.270 --> 00:08:09.930
and working together to feed
forward what we're learning.

00:08:09.930 --> 00:08:11.970
But solar sailing isn't only confined

00:08:11.970 --> 00:08:13.200
to the inner solar system

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:14.830
where there's plenty of sunlight.

00:08:14.830 --> 00:08:16.033
Quite the opposite.

00:08:17.020 --> 00:08:18.530
It could offer the best way

00:08:18.530 --> 00:08:21.250
to reach the outer
solar system and beyond.

00:08:21.250 --> 00:08:24.050
But it will need to improve
from its current technology.

00:08:26.220 --> 00:08:27.370
One of the main disadvantages

00:08:27.370 --> 00:08:29.660
with current solar sail designs

00:08:29.660 --> 00:08:32.140
is that they are monolithic,

00:08:32.140 --> 00:08:35.180
which means that you
ultimately have to pitch,

00:08:35.180 --> 00:08:38.230
roll and yaw the entire sail.

00:08:38.230 --> 00:08:42.040
But if you can break the
sail up into component parts,

00:08:42.040 --> 00:08:44.340
you can then individually articulate

00:08:44.340 --> 00:08:45.760
each of those components

00:08:45.760 --> 00:08:48.250
and you can gain a lot
more maneuverability.

00:08:48.250 --> 00:08:50.350
And that's what a company called Xplore

00:08:50.350 --> 00:08:54.470
are working on with their SunVane concept.

00:08:54.470 --> 00:08:57.250
SunVane takes a really large sail area

00:08:57.250 --> 00:09:01.340
and breaks it up into six
or eight individual vanes.

00:09:01.340 --> 00:09:04.160
The vanes can then be
independently articulated.

00:09:04.160 --> 00:09:07.960
That would make SunVane a
highly maneuverable sail.

00:09:07.960 --> 00:09:10.590
And the advantages of high maneuverability

00:09:10.590 --> 00:09:13.770
mean that you can reach
a target destination

00:09:13.770 --> 00:09:14.990
that much faster,

00:09:14.990 --> 00:09:16.950
because you can plot a more direct

00:09:16.950 --> 00:09:19.000
and a more efficient course

00:09:19.000 --> 00:09:20.913
to get you from point A to point B.

00:09:23.490 --> 00:09:25.910
The SunVane will tack
inward toward the sun,

00:09:25.910 --> 00:09:28.200
like a sailboat tacking into the wind,

00:09:28.200 --> 00:09:29.260
and then turn its vanes

00:09:29.260 --> 00:09:31.513
toward the sun for maximum acceleration.

00:09:32.670 --> 00:09:34.280
The technology demonstration,

00:09:34.280 --> 00:09:36.590
which is still in the
initial planning stages,

00:09:36.590 --> 00:09:39.620
aims to reach speeds of
5 to 8 astronomical units

00:09:39.620 --> 00:09:41.216
or AU per year.

00:09:41.216 --> 00:09:45.040
One AU being the average distance
from the Earth to the Sun.

00:09:45.040 --> 00:09:47.100
That will make the SunVane
demonstration mission

00:09:47.100 --> 00:09:50.080
the fastest man-made probe ever launched,

00:09:50.080 --> 00:09:52.170
reaching Jupiter in less than a year.

00:09:52.170 --> 00:09:53.643
All without a drop of fuel.

00:09:57.630 --> 00:09:59.980
But considering the
great distances of space,

00:09:59.980 --> 00:10:02.000
it will need to go even faster

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:03.610
and fly even closer to the sun

00:10:03.610 --> 00:10:05.630
to accomplish the kind
of deep space missions

00:10:05.630 --> 00:10:07.493
scientists are hoping to achieve.

00:10:08.330 --> 00:10:11.220
But so far the technology isn't there.

00:10:11.220 --> 00:10:12.930
Well, the temperatures in that environment

00:10:12.930 --> 00:10:14.420
are going to be extremely high.

00:10:14.420 --> 00:10:19.420
So you need very lightweight
yet very durable materials.

00:10:19.890 --> 00:10:22.240
So some advanced materials
that might come along

00:10:22.240 --> 00:10:24.800
might be something like silicon nitride

00:10:24.800 --> 00:10:26.820
or silica sails.

00:10:26.820 --> 00:10:29.430
These materials don't yet exist,

00:10:29.430 --> 00:10:32.430
but there are companies that
are working on developing them.

00:10:33.430 --> 00:10:35.630
Another limitation is that solar sails

00:10:35.630 --> 00:10:38.863
won't easily be able to carry
anything heavy, like a person.

00:10:40.260 --> 00:10:43.200
To propel a larger payload,

00:10:43.200 --> 00:10:45.900
say a crude payload,

00:10:45.900 --> 00:10:49.390
well, you're going to
need a very large sail

00:10:49.390 --> 00:10:54.390
and a lot of time to get to
a sufficient enough speed

00:10:54.800 --> 00:10:57.050
unless, of course, you
wanna send your crew

00:10:57.050 --> 00:10:59.860
into a sun-dive maneuver.

00:10:59.860 --> 00:11:02.543
But that might not be very
good for their health.

00:11:07.170 --> 00:11:08.250
But for smaller craft,

00:11:08.250 --> 00:11:10.513
the possibilities are limitless.

00:11:11.900 --> 00:11:14.200
The most ambitious
mission using solar sails

00:11:14.200 --> 00:11:16.403
even involves visiting
another solar system,

00:11:17.350 --> 00:11:19.680
and would allow us to reach
our nearest star system,

00:11:19.680 --> 00:11:22.213
Alpha Centauri, in just 20 years.

00:11:23.400 --> 00:11:25.733
It's called Breakthrough Starshot.

00:11:26.609 --> 00:11:31.430
So Starshot envisions using
an array of terawatt lasers,

00:11:31.430 --> 00:11:35.430
concentrating their
light on a tiny sailcraft

00:11:35.430 --> 00:11:37.313
that weighs less than one gram.

00:11:38.170 --> 00:11:40.900
In that one-gram package
contains the sail,

00:11:40.900 --> 00:11:45.390
spacecraft, a camera,
a transmission antenna,

00:11:45.390 --> 00:11:48.290
everything, all hyper miniaturized.

00:11:48.290 --> 00:11:50.940
The NanoSails would accelerate
at something like 10%

00:11:50.940 --> 00:11:52.150
the speed of light

00:11:52.150 --> 00:11:56.023
and potentially reach Alpha
Centauri in about 20 years.

00:11:57.520 --> 00:11:58.960
While the technology is nowhere near

00:11:58.960 --> 00:12:01.100
making Breakthrough Starshot a reality,

00:12:01.100 --> 00:12:03.593
solar sailing is entering its next phase.

00:12:05.390 --> 00:12:07.130
Missions that were once huge endeavors

00:12:07.130 --> 00:12:08.980
because of the cost and size

00:12:08.980 --> 00:12:11.490
will soon be able to launch
as secondary payloads,

00:12:11.490 --> 00:12:13.123
piggybacking off other missions.

00:12:14.800 --> 00:12:18.280
That means visiting asteroids,
moons of the outer planets

00:12:18.280 --> 00:12:19.670
or even interstellar objects

00:12:19.670 --> 00:12:21.210
that enter our solar system

00:12:21.210 --> 00:12:23.053
could be achieved much more easily.

00:12:24.900 --> 00:12:27.390
You get an infinite amount of sunlight

00:12:27.390 --> 00:12:28.850
that is never ending,

00:12:28.850 --> 00:12:31.370
so you leave your fuel on the ground

00:12:31.370 --> 00:12:33.430
and you just continue to sail.

00:12:33.430 --> 00:12:35.860
So it's not like sailing by sunlight

00:12:35.860 --> 00:12:38.280
is going to replace chemical rockets,

00:12:38.280 --> 00:12:41.690
but solar sailing will allow us to do more

00:12:41.690 --> 00:12:44.323
than what we can do with
chemical rockets alone.

00:12:46.880 --> 00:12:48.980
This technology is important
for future missions

00:12:48.980 --> 00:12:51.490
because it's an enabling technology.

00:12:51.490 --> 00:12:54.440
It enables you to do missions

00:12:54.440 --> 00:12:56.920
with solar-weather monitoring

00:12:56.920 --> 00:12:58.180
that you couldn't do otherwise,

00:12:58.180 --> 00:13:01.660
poles sitting on planets that
you couldn't do otherwise,

00:13:01.660 --> 00:13:03.200
doing multiple encounters

00:13:03.200 --> 00:13:06.654
and having a flexibility to adapt

00:13:06.654 --> 00:13:07.890
that you couldn't do otherwise.

00:13:07.890 --> 00:13:10.680
And someday maybe doing
interstellar missions,

00:13:10.680 --> 00:13:12.333
which you couldn't do otherwise.

